      "summary": "Middleware moves bulky context into files, summaries, and isolated tasks while keeping evidence retrievable.",
      "body": [
        "LangChain describes five context-management techniques in its Deep Agents harness: offloading large inputs, summarizing history, loading skills on demand, isolating work in subagents, and persisting memory in files. Middleware applies these mechanisms at points around model and tool calls. Large tool results become previews with file references; older conversation messages can become a summary while recent messages remain intact. The post emphasizes retaining the underlying history so an agent can recover details later. Its walkthrough explains implementation choices rather than reporting a controlled accuracy or latency experiment.",
        "This matters when an incident depends on information that disappeared from the active prompt. Our editorial recommendation is to retain links between a model call, the context transformation that preceded it, and the stored source material. Replay tasks requiring a detail from before compaction, and inspect both successful retrieval and forgotten constraints. Compare task outcomes and token use across the same workload before changing thresholds. A smaller context window is useful only when the agent can still recover the evidence needed for its next decision."
